\begin_body

\begin_layout Standard
Choosing the pure bare-metal path means implementing the complete,
 low-level serial frame protocol required to configure the external Infineon CYW43439 chip over a shared SPI-like layout.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the CYW43439 relies on a dynamic host bus interface,
 setting a simple LED state requires communicating with its inner gSPI (generic SPI) module.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the Pico 2 W this interface is wired directly to specific microchip pins rather than a standard peripheral controller meaning that raw SVD registers must be used to manually bit-bang the communication sequences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Phase 1:
 Microcontroller Hardware Pin Mapping
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To control the external chip,
 configure the following four pins via the RP2350.GPIO SVD layout:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GPIO 23 (WL_REG_ON):
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Power control/Hardware reset must be set as an Output.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GPIO 25 (WL_CS):
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Chip Select must be set as an Output (Active Low).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GPIO 29 (WL_CLK):
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 SPI Clock line must be set as an Output.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GPIO 24 (WL_DIO):
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

A bi-directional data stream that dynamically toggles between Output (sending frames) and Input (reading status).
\end_layout

